      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The work order number is usually assigned by the council's street lighting department when they receive your report. You can try checking your email or the council's online portal to see if they've assigned a work order number to your report. If you can't find it, you can try contacting the council's customer service department and ask them to look up your report.\n\nAs for the rhythmic flickering, that's a bit more unusual. It's possible that the bulb is faulty, but it's also possible that there's something more going on. You might want to ask the council's street lighting department to send someone to investigate. They might need to check the lamp's electrical connections or the wiring to determine the cause of the flickering.\n\nIt's a bit like trying to track down a mischievous goblin who's playing tricks on you. You need to follow the clues and gather more information to figure out what's going on. But don't worry, with persistence and the right resources, you'll be able to get to the bottom of the issue and get the lamp fixed.\n\nBy the way, have you ever heard of the \"Goblin's Gambit\" in chess? It's a bit of a tricky maneuver, but it can",
